## Hosts and URLs

- Default identity is `HOST_PRIMARY` in `.env`. Extra hosts need a `routing.HostName` const in `config/hosts.go` plus a matching `App.Hosts` entry (load `HOST_<NAME>` yourself — not scaffolded).
- Declare the host on the route value with `routing.Host(config.HostAdmin)` and register it with `rt.Host(config.HostAdmin).Get(route.Pattern(), handler)`. Build the registry with `routing.NewHosts(cfg.App.Hosts)`, pass it to `anduin.WithHosts`, and pass the same value to `FullURL(hosts, ...)`. Unknown hosts are served by the primary mux.
- Host-scoped policy reads `anduin.RequestHost(ctx)`, the `routing.HostName` the dispatcher resolved (case-insensitive, aliases, configured bare hostname matching any port, unknown → primary); never re-parse `anduin.Request(ctx).Host`. `hosts.Resolve(authority)` exposes the same resolution. A host with no registered routes still reports its name but dispatches to the primary mux.
- Cross-host navigation uses a plain `<a href>`; Inertia `<Link>` is only for same-host visits. Generated helpers return absolute URLs for host-bound routes.
- `/api` stays on the primary host unless those routes set `Host(...)`. Layout CSS/JS use `FullURL(hosts, ...)` to primary.
- Never invent frontend URLs in Inertia apps: import `resources/js/routes.ts` after `andurel sync routes --json`. Helpers are generated for routes marked `routing.TypeScriptRoute()` and return full URLs once `configureRouteHosts` runs from the shared `hosts` prop at boot. Call helpers at render time, not in module-scope constants, and use `routes.name.path()` for a relative path.
- After changing handler page props or request payload structs, run `andurel sync payloads --strict` so `resources/js/types/payloads.ts` stays in sync. It fails (exit 5) on a `FromStruct` argument it cannot trace, after writing the file; pass named composite literals, typed variables, or typed function parameters to `FromStruct`.

## Composition

`internal/runtime` constructs the graph explicitly, bottom-up: telemetry → database → domains → services → renderer/handlers/middleware → router. `cmd/app` and `cmd/queue` only load env, create the signal context, and start `runtime`. Add a dependency by constructing it and passing it as a constructor argument — never through a container.

## Migrations

`cmd/migrate` applies pending SQL with `storage.RunMigrations` on `migrations.Migrations`. It is a one-shot process, not part of `cmd/app` start. Development uses `andurel db migrate up`.
